---
import BaseHead from "../components/BaseHead.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import { SITE_TITLE } from "../consts";

const description =
    "About José María Sánchez Salas — Software Engineer & Architect building and evolving complex software systems.";
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
    <head>
        <BaseHead title={`About — ${SITE_TITLE}`} description={description} />
    </head>

    <body>
        <Header />

        <main>
            <section class="page-intro">
                <h1>I TEND TO END UP WHERE THINGS GET COMPLICATED.</h1>

                <p class="lead">
                    I'm José María Sánchez Salas, a Software Engineer and
                    Architect with around eight years of experience building and
                    evolving software systems.
                </p>

                <p>
                    I started as a backend developer. Over time, the interesting
                    problems moved further away from individual services and
                    closer to the system itself: architecture, data flows,
                    performance, infrastructure, constraints and the decisions
                    that determine whether a system can keep evolving.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section class="page-section">
                <h2>THE ENVIRONMENTS CHANGED. THE PROBLEM DIDN'T.</h2>

                <p>
                    I've worked on backend platforms, APIs, distributed
                    processing systems and real-time data pipelines.
                </p>

                <p>
                    I've also worked much closer to the physical world:
                    industrial edge systems running on constrained hardware,
                    communicating through protocols like MQTT, Modbus, TCP, UDP
                    and ADS, deployed at customer sites where connectivity,
                    resources and failure modes were very different from a
                    typical cloud environment.
                </p>

                <p>Those systems look very different on paper.</p>

                <p>But they tend to create the same underlying problem:</p>

                <p class="statement">
                    <strong>
                        There is more complexity than can be understood by
                        looking at one piece of the system at a time.
                    </strong>
                </p>
            </section>

            <section class="page-section">
                <h2>THAT'S WHAT I LIKE WORKING ON.</h2>

                <p>
                    I enjoy figuring out how the pieces actually fit together.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Where the boundaries should be. Which constraints are real.
                    What needs to happen in real time. What happens when
                    something fails. Which abstractions are useful and which
                    ones are just adding another layer of complexity.
                </p>

                <p>
                    That usually means going deeper than the initial problem
                    suggests.
                </p>

                <p>
                    A performance problem might actually be a data-model
                    problem. A scaling problem might actually be a
                    workload-management problem. A messy codebase might actually
                    be suffering from unclear boundaries.
                </p>

                <p class="statement">
                    <strong>
                        I like finding the problem underneath the problem.
                    </strong>
                </p>
            </section>

            <section class="page-section">
                <h2>ARCHITECTURE WITHOUT LEAVING ENGINEERING BEHIND.</h2>

                <p>
                    Over the years, my role moved from implementing individual
                    services to taking responsibility for how larger systems fit
                    together.
                </p>

                <p>
                    I've designed systems from scratch, evolved existing
                    architectures, investigated difficult performance problems,
                    changed database models, worked with constrained hardware
                    and integrated systems that don't behave like ordinary
                    software.
                </p>

                <p>
                    I've also led small engineering teams and worked closely
                    with other engineers to turn ambiguous problems into
                    something concrete enough to build.
                </p>

                <p>I still write code.</p>

                <p>
                    For me, architecture isn't about moving away from
                    engineering.
                </p>

                <p class="statement">
                    <strong>
                        It's about making better engineering decisions before —
                        and while — the code is being written.
                    </strong>
                </p>
            </section>

            <section class="page-section">
                <h2>
                    A COUPLE OF SYSTEMS THAT REPRESENT THE KIND OF WORK I ENJOY.
                </h2>

                <div class="work-list">
                    <article>
                        <h3>AKVAKT Industrial Edge Platform</h3>

                        <p>
                            A reusable industrial platform built from
                            customer-specific prototypes, running at real
                            customer sites with constrained hardware, real-time
                            data, industrial protocols and remote operation.
                        </p>

                        <a href="/notebook/akvakt">Read the technical story →</a
                        >
                    </article>

                    <article>
                        <h3>CIXOR</h3>

                        <p>
                            A workload management system for large real-time
                            indexing workloads, where the challenge was changing
                            the processing model without breaking the systems
                            around it.
                        </p>

                        <a href="/notebook/cixor">Read the technical story →</a>
                    </article>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="page-section">
                <h2>THE ENGINEERING NOTEBOOK.</h2>

                <p>
                    I started the Engineering Notebook to document the reasoning
                    behind this kind of work.
                </p>

                <p>Not tutorials. Not technology lists.</p>

                <p>
                    Technical stories about real systems: what made them
                    difficult, which constraints mattered, what decisions were
                    made, what went wrong, and why the architecture ended up the
                    way it did.
                </p>

                <a href="/notebook">Explore the Engineering Notebook →</a>
            </section>

            <section class="page-section contact">
                <h2>IF THE SYSTEM IS DIFFICULT, I'M INTERESTED.</h2>

                <p>
                    If you're dealing with a system that's becoming difficult to
                    understand, a technical decision that needs direction, or an
                    engineering problem that needs someone to take ownership,
                    let's talk.
                </p>

                <a href="/contact">Get in touch →</a>
            </section>
        </main>

        <Footer />
    </body>
</html>
